  "notes": "A Latin-pop balladeer who crossed into Euro-dance-pop, modelled on the ballad wing. The section's own boundary rule sends club-groove records to `dance-pop`, so what stays here is the 82–100 ballad band, which the research pairs with a half-time backbeat on 3 — `halfTime` true, snare `halftime_3`. The 122–133 four-on-the-floor band is the club engine and is named here rather than encoded. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"a soft kick on 1/3\", \"the snare on 3\", and \"a 16th shaker\", which is the shaker-led perc block; the sourced single also puts the fill vocabulary at almost nothing, so the schedule is wide and the hat rolls near zero. Harmony is the stated i–VI–III–VII ballad cycle with a IV–V–I chorus lift, plus the \"frequent final-chorus semitone modulation\" which the model expresses as an arrangement habit rather than a chord. The melody is \"breathy and low in the verse, chest-belt on the chorus apex\", i.e. a wide register with an arch-dominant contour. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says so: only catalogue tempo readings are available and the drum grammar is convention-derived. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and `bpm.mode` 90. ⚠ **`bassline.rhythm` is `boom_chick`, not `reese_sustain`** — the research names the figure outright: *“sustained synth bass roots on the ballads; on the dance material an 8th-note filtered bass locked under the kick”*. A held `reese_sustain` writes one note per chord and says the opposite of what the entry describes.",
